{
 "generatedBy": "aggregated by hand from `node tools/cold_tier_perf.mjs --out` runs (one per point); the harness emits per-run reports, not this cross-point shape, so this file is DERIVED from those reports rather than written by the harness. Every `blankP` - the value the gate is decided on - is recomputed from the counts recorded beside it by tests/test_cold_tier.py - ThresholdChoiceTests. `fmpP` and `ttiP` are carried through from the harness's own statistics for context; this file does NOT record the direction counts those two are computed from, so they are reported rather than reproducible here, and nothing is decided on them.",
 "recordedAt": "2026-08-11",
 "platform": "win32 x64, 16 x AMD EPYC 7763, node v24.18.0, chromium 149.0.7827.55",
 "alpha": 0.05,
 "screenIterations": 10,
 "iterations": 10,
 "howToRerun": {
  "cwd": "plugins/commentable-html/dev",
  "prerequisite": "python tools/build.py --assets-dir assets --out-dir skill --pkg-dir ../pkg/skills/commentable-html --examples-dir ../examples (the harness stages its corpus from skill/dist/SHAREABLE.html)",
  "screen": [
   "node tools/cold_tier_perf.mjs --iterations 10 --keep-rows 20 --min-rows 40",
   "node tools/cold_tier_perf.mjs --iterations 10 --keep-rows 100 --min-rows 200",
   "node tools/cold_tier_perf.mjs --iterations 10 --keep-rows 250 --min-rows 500",
   "node tools/cold_tier_perf.mjs --iterations 10 --keep-rows 500 --min-rows 1000",
   "node tools/cold_tier_perf.mjs --iterations 10 --keep-rows 1000 --min-rows 2000",
   "node tools/cold_tier_perf.mjs --iterations 10 --keep-rows 2000 --min-rows 4000"
  ],
  "confirm": [
   "node tools/cold_tier_perf.mjs --iterations 30 --keep-rows 20 --min-rows 40",
   "node tools/cold_tier_perf.mjs --iterations 30 --keep-rows 1000 --min-rows 2000",
   "node tools/cold_tier_perf.mjs --iterations 30 --keep-rows 2000 --min-rows 4000"
  ],
  "harness": "dev/tools/cold_tier_perf.mjs, added by issue #1271 (CMH-COLD-09)"
 },
 "metric": "blankP is the exact one-sided sign-test p over the DISCORDANT paired loads - the iterations where exactly one variant painted nothing until the parser had finished (first paint at or after domInteractive). The paint distribution here is BIMODAL - a load either paints in a few hundred ms or not until parse end, seconds later - so the blank RATE describes the reader's experience and a median does not: at 20/40 the largest document's median cold paint is 4952 ms precisely because most loads fell into the late mode. fmpP (the paired FMP sign test) and ttiP are recorded beside it but are NOT the gate: fmpP's smallest value anywhere in the screen is 0.1719, so gating on it would pass the shipped 20/40 pair that demonstrably regresses paint by seconds.",
 "metricCaveat": "The blank test is `fmp >= domInteractive` and the tier moves BOTH terms - it parses less DOM, so domInteractive arrives earlier too - which means a load can be counted blank with its paint time unchanged. Two things keep that from hollowing out the gate: the paint TIME is recorded beside every rate, and the distribution is bimodal rather than shifted, so the counted loads are real not-until-parse loads. The GATE is the blank rate; paint time and TTI are reported beside it as corroboration, not as additional pass conditions.",
 "divergencesFromHarness": [
  "A DECLINED document (both variants byte-identical) is recorded as unmeasured and EXCLUDED; cold_tier_perf.mjs FAILS it outright ('nothing was measured'). Excluding it is what lets a pair that disengages a document be judged on the documents it still compresses.",
  "The harness's paired FMP sign test is NOT used as a pass condition here: on this data it has no discriminating power (smallest p in the screen is 0.1719), so it would pass the very defaults this change exists to replace. It is recorded per document as fmpP.",
  "The harness's TTI budget is not re-imposed as a pass condition; dTti and ttiP are recorded instead. Every confirmed point is far BETTER than plain on the largest document (-1338 to -1517 ms cold), so the budget never binds and gating on it would add nothing."
 ],
 "powerNote": "An exact sign test cannot reject at alpha=0.05 below 5 discordant pairs (2^-4 = 0.0625), and that floor is a property of the DISCORDANT count, not of the iteration count: more iterations help only if discordant pairs scale with them. At the chosen pair the largest document produced 1 discordant pair in 30, so its p=0.5 is pinned by construction and carries no evidential weight on its own. What the sample does support is a BOUND: see `residual`.",
 "rule": "TWO STAGES. Stage 1 SCREENS six keep_rows/min_rows pairs over the whole corpus at 10 iterations; a declined document measures nothing and is excluded rather than counted as a pass. Stage 2 CONFIRMS on a FRESH sample at 30 iterations. The GATE, at both stages, is the paired blank-until-parsed sign test against the PLAIN document (not against another pair), cold AND warm, on every MEASURED document, at alpha=0.05; the chosen default is the smallest keepRows that clears it at the CONFIRMATION, because keepRows and the compression win move in opposite directions. Note the asymmetry deliberately: the screen's REJECTIONS were not re-measured, while its non-rejections were. Every rejected pair was rejected BY THE GATE (100/200, 250/500 and 500/1000 all record blankP <= 0.03125 and paintPasses false on the largest document), and each also carries a paired cold paint delta of +2214 to +4866 ms - an effect one to two orders of magnitude above this measurement's noise floor, which is what makes those rejections safe to accept from a 10-iteration sample. A non-rejection at 10 iterations carries almost no information, which is why only those were re-measured. So 500/1000 (which would have saved 321,507 more bytes) was rejected by the gate, with its paint delta corroborating that the rejection is not a small-sample artifact.",
 "residual": "This is NOT a demonstration of parity, and the gate being cleared is not one either. At the chosen pair the largest document still painted nothing until parsed on 1 of 30 cold loads against 0 of 30 plain. The one-sided 95% Clopper-Pearson bound on that rate is 14.9%, so the data are consistent with as many as roughly 1 cold load in 7 still blanking; this design has only 17.5% power against a true 10% rate and 47.6% against 15%. The quoted count is also the MINIMUM of the three confirmed pairs, so it is biased low. Treat the decision as a judgement made on a measured improvement (19 of 30 down to 1 of 30, and a +4608 ms paired cold paint regression down to no detectable change) with the residual bounded above, NOT as a proof of non-inferiority: establishing that would need a stated margin and a much larger sample (about 60 iterations at 0 discordant pairs to bound the rate under 10%). What the earlier draft of this file got wrong is instructive: 1000/2000 'passed' at 10 iterations (3 of 10, p=0.125) and FAILED at 30 (10 of 30, p=0.00098).",
 "residualBound": {
  "document": "xl-tree",
  "state": "cold",
  "blankOn": 1,
  "iterations": 30,
  "upperBound95": 0.1486,
  "powerAgainst10Percent": 0.175,
  "powerAgainst15Percent": 0.476
 },
 "nullControl": "A point where the tier DECLINES compares a file with ITSELF, so every difference it shows is this measurement's false-positive floor - and it is not small: those byte-identical comparisons reach blankP 0.0625 (sm-report warm at 1000/2000) and ttiP 0.0107 (md-report warm at 250/500). A verdict anywhere near alpha therefore carries little information, which is the second reason the chosen pair's p=0.5 is reported as a bound rather than as proof.",
 "drift": "Within a point, ON/OFF order is counterbalanced, so a machine that drifts through a run does not favour either variant. BETWEEN points it is not: the six screen points ran back to back over about 50 minutes and the three confirmation points over about 70 minutes on a shared machine, in ascending keepRows order. The between-point comparison that ranks the pairs is therefore uncontrolled for drift, and the null-control rows above show how large that noise can be. The confirmation re-measured the candidates on a fresh sample, which is what makes the ranking between the three confirmed pairs trustworthy; the screen's ranking is not relied on for anything but elimination on effects far above the noise.",
 "coverageLimit": "The corpus bodies are 200, 900 and 11500 rows. At the chosen pair the two smaller documents DECLINE, so the entire measured result rests on the 11500-row body - which is 2.9x the gate it sets. NO document with a body between 900 and 11500 rows was measured, so the paint behaviour of a document just over min_rows (the smallest the shipped default will now compress, at 4001 rows) is EXTRAPOLATED, not observed. keepRows and minRows were also held at a fixed 1:2 ratio throughout, so nothing here isolates the effect of minRows alone.",
 "chosen": {
  "keepRows": 2000,
  "minRows": 4000
 },
 "baseline": {
  "keepRows": 20,
  "minRows": 40
 },
